  "aiOverview": {
    "content": " The Saint Louis University mascot is the Billiken, a mythical good-luck figure with a pointed head, wide grin, and pointed ears, representing \"things as they ought to be\". Created in 1908 by art teacher Florence Pretz, the Billiken became SLU's official mascot around 1910, often associated with a smiling, joyful appearance. Key details about the SLU Billiken: Origin: Created by Florence Pretz in 1908, it was a popular good luck charm, often used as a doll or figurine according to Newswise. Connection to SLU: It became associated with the university around 1910, when a law student/student-artist compared the university's football coach, John Bender, to the figure say the Saint Louis University archives. Uniqueness: SLU is the only university with a Billiken mascot according to Newswise. Appearance: The mascot has undergone several design changes, including a 2015 update, often appearing with a large, joyful grin say the Saint Louis University archives. Other St. Louis Mascots: University of Missouri–St. Louis (UMSL): The mascot is Louie the Triton, adopted in 2007.",
